<main>
  <section>
    <p>
      I'm a second-year Ph.D. student in the Computer Science Department at
      <a href="https://www.stanford.edu">Stanford University</a>, where I'm
      fortunate to be advised by <a href="#">Prof. Alex Chen</a>. I'm affiliated
      with the <a href="#">Stanford AI Lab</a> and the <a href="#">Stanford NLP Group</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Before Stanford, I completed my B.S. in Computer Science and Mathematics
      at MIT, where I worked with Prof. Regina Barzilay on applications of deep
      learning to computational biology. I also spent time as a research intern
      at Google DeepMind and Meta AI.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Research Interests</h2>
    <p>
      Broadly, I'm interested in understanding how deep learning systems work
      and making them more reliable. My current research focuses on:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Interpretability:</strong> Understanding what neural networks
      have learned and how they make decisions</li>
      <li><strong>Robustness:</strong> Making models more reliable under
      distribution shift and adversarial conditions</li>
      <li><strong>Optimization:</strong> Theoretical analysis of training
      dynamics in deep networks</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

